WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.310
Case Study:

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.129
URL Shortener and SMS Short Links.

3
00:00:04.409 --> 00:00:08.633
Chapter 38, from the Case Studies part of the System Design Playbook.

4
00:00:10.383 --> 00:00:11.343
At a glance:

5
00:00:11.623 --> 00:00:12.796
Chapter at a glance.

6
00:00:13.076 --> 00:00:14.079
Reader bands:

7
00:00:14.359 --> 00:00:16.663
B1, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:16.943 --> 00:00:22.404
Ch 02, Ch 07, Ch 11, Ch 20, What you will build:

9
00:00:22.684 --> 00:00:25.927
The grocery platform's short-link service, gro.cr.

10
00:00:26.207 --> 00:00:31.775
It carries every order-tracking, slot-reminder and refund SMS, plus marketing campaigns.

11
00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:45.068
This is the classic first interview case study, done properly: requirements, estimation, code generation, the redirect path, caching under campaign spikes, click analytics, abuse prevention and expiry.

12
00:00:45.348 --> 00:00:50.127
Four runnable labs, one with a real HTTP server on PostgreSQL.

13
00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:53.055
L1: minting codes.

14
00:00:53.335 --> 00:01:01.655
Over 2 million codes, 6-character truncated MD5 hashes produced 40 collisions (the birthday bound predicts 35).

15
00:01:01.935 --> 00:01:08.570
A plain counter gives 5-character codes, but 100% of their neighbours are other customers' live links.

16
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:18.429
A counter passed through a keyed Feistel permutation is unique by construction, needs no lookup, and neighbour guesses hit 0.00%.

17
00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.464
L2: a real shortener.

18
00:01:21.744 --> 00:01:25.136
JDK HttpServer and PostgreSQL.

19
00:01:25.416 --> 00:01:35.379
A retried create returned the same code. http, a grocery.in@evil.example userinfo trick and a look-alike domain were all refused.

20
00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.416
An expired link returned 410 and an unknown one 404.

21
00:01:40.696 --> 00:01:45.731
Under 50,000 Zipf-distributed redirects the cache absorbed 85%.

22
00:01:46.011 --> 00:01:52.987
716 of the database reads were concurrent misses on the same cold code, a small thundering herd.

23
00:01:54.337 --> 00:01:55.873
L3: the read path.

24
00:01:56.153 --> 00:02:06.926
An LRU cache holding 1% of links served 58.5% of 10 million clicks; 10x the memory reached only 77.7%.

25
00:02:07.206 --> 00:02:16.017
A 20-million-SMS campaign put 27,184 req per second on one Redis shard, 49x its peers.

26
00:02:16.297 --> 00:02:20.414
A 1-second near-cache on each node cut that to 570.

27
00:02:20.694 --> 00:02:22.294
L4: counting clicks.

28
00:02:22.574 --> 00:02:34.521
On PostgreSQL with durable commits, a per-click UPDATE on one hot campaign row ran at ~2,300 clicks per second with a ~67 ms p99.

29
00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:42.523
Micro-batching made the redirect's cost a 0.01 ms memory increment and used 20x fewer statements.

30
00:02:42.803 --> 00:02:58.953
Merged HyperLogLog sketches counted 663,668 unique devices within +0.41% in 16 KB each; adding per-node counts overstated them by 294%.

31
00:03:00.303 --> 00:03:01.284
Reading time:

32
00:03:01.564 --> 00:03:03.185
100 min + code.

33
00:03:04.535 --> 00:03:05.389
Part 1.

34
00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:07.589
Introduction and Why It Matters.

35
00:03:08.839 --> 00:03:11.228
A story: the link that took down checkout.

36
00:03:11.508 --> 00:03:23.924
The grocery platform's first short-link service was a weekend project: a links table with an auto-increment id, base62-encoded, and a controller that looked it up and returned a 301.

37
00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:25.313
It worked for a year.

38
00:03:25.593 --> 00:03:28.217
Then four things happened in one Diwali week:

39
00:03:28.497 --> 00:03:29.500
Enumeration.

40
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:39.849
A security researcher noticed that gro.cr or 6laZE and gro.cr or 6laZF were both order-tracking links, for different customers.

41
00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:44.119
Each tracking page showed the delivery address and the rider's phone number.

42
00:03:44.399 --> 00:03:48.772
Sequential codes had turned every SMS into a key to its neighbours.

43
00:03:50.122 --> 00:03:51.338
The campaign spike.

44
00:03:51.618 --> 00:03:56.013
Marketing sent 20 million SMS with one offer link at 7 pm.

45
00:03:56.293 --> 00:04:00.090
Every click hit the same row, the same Redis key, the same shard.

46
00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:09.991
The redirect service's connection pool, shared with the order-tracking API, was exhausted, and customers opening where is my order? links saw errors.

47
00:04:10.271 --> 00:04:11.423
Analytics lied.

48
00:04:11.703 --> 00:04:17.293
The dashboard showed 40% fewer clicks than the SMS gateway's delivery count suggested.

49
00:04:17.573 --> 00:04:22.970
The 301 redirects were cached by browsers: repeat clicks never reached the server.

50
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.879
At the same time WhatsApp's link-preview crawler inflated the clicks on shared links.

51
00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:30.040
Phishing.

52
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.437
Anyone with an internal API token could shorten any URL.

53
00:04:34.717 --> 00:04:44.168
A compromised partner account created gro.cr or kyc pointing at a look-alike login page, and the platform's own trusted domain delivered it.

54
00:04:44.448 --> 00:04:56.608
The rebuilt service, which this chapter designs, treats a short link as what it is: a tiny, public, security-sensitive, read-heavy key-value lookup with spiky traffic and analytics on the side.

55
00:04:57.958 --> 00:04:58.641
Quote:

56
00:04:58.921 --> 00:05:00.585
The thesis of this chapter.

57
00:05:00.865 --> 00:05:10.721
A URL shortener looks trivial, a map from code to URL, which is why interviewers love it: all the difficulty is in the details that production adds.

58
00:05:11.001 --> 00:05:17.337
Code generation is a trade-off between coordination, collisions, length and guessability (L1).

59
00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:24.742
Writes must be idempotent and validated, because retries are normal and a trusted domain is a phishing tool (L2).

60
00:05:25.022 --> 00:05:36.947
The read path is a caching problem with a hot-key twist: skewed popularity makes a small cache effective, but a campaign concentrates load on one key that sharding cannot spread (L3).

61
00:05:37.227 --> 00:05:43.499
Analytics must never slow the redirect: count asynchronously, and use sketches for unique counts (L4).

62
00:05:43.779 --> 00:05:47.107
Get those four right and the simple service survives Diwali.

63
00:05:48.457 --> 00:05:50.761
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

64
00:05:51.041 --> 00:05:52.385
Here is a quick table:

65
00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:55.823
Producer of links, and Volume, and Lifetime, and Notes.

66
00:05:56.103 --> 00:06:01.713
Notification service, order tracking, OTP-free deep links, slot reminders, refunds.

67
00:06:01.993 --> 00:06:03.999
Volume: ~2M per day.

68
00:06:04.279 --> 00:06:05.196
Lifetime:

69
00:06:05.476 --> 00:06:06.500
30 days.

70
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:07.591
Notes:

71
00:06:07.871 --> 00:06:10.815
Personal: the page behind must still authenticate.

72
00:06:11.095 --> 00:06:14.785
Marketing, campaign links, one per campaign or per recipient.

73
00:06:15.065 --> 00:06:15.897
Volume:

74
00:06:16.177 --> 00:06:17.969
1 to 20M per campaign.

75
00:06:18.249 --> 00:06:19.167
Lifetime:

76
00:06:19.447 --> 00:06:20.492
90 days.

77
00:06:20.772 --> 00:06:21.583
Notes:

78
00:06:21.863 --> 00:06:25.404
Personalised links for attribution; UTM parameters.

79
00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:27.753
Support, links in chat and email.

80
00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:30.081
Volume: ~50k per day.

81
00:06:30.361 --> 00:06:31.279
Lifetime:

82
00:06:31.559 --> 00:06:32.604
90 days.

83
00:06:32.884 --> 00:06:35.615
Partners or vendors, store pages, offers.

84
00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.727
Volume:

85
00:06:37.007 --> 00:06:37.732
Low.

86
00:06:38.012 --> 00:06:38.929
Lifetime:

87
00:06:39.209 --> 00:06:40.297
Until revoked.

88
00:06:40.577 --> 00:06:41.388
Notes:

89
00:06:41.668 --> 00:06:43.737
Allow-listed destinations only.

90
00:06:45.087 --> 00:06:51.509
The service sits behind the SMS pipeline (Ch 40) and in front of the web and app landing pages.

91
00:06:51.789 --> 00:07:11.927
India's SMS rules make it part of compliance too: links in commercial and transactional SMS must be on the sender's registered, whitelisted URLs in the DLT system (TRAI, from late 2024, VERIFY current rules), so a controlled first-party shortener is effectively mandatory.

92
00:07:13.277 --> 00:07:14.600
Learning objectives.

93
00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.779
By the end you will be able to:

94
00:07:17.059 --> 00:07:25.613
Run the interview framework on a classic problem: requirements, estimation, API, data model, high-level design, deep dives.

95
00:07:25.893 --> 00:07:33.360
Choose a code-generation scheme and justify it with collision, coordination, length and privacy numbers (L1).

96
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:40.339
Build the write path: idempotent creation, destination validation, custom aliases, expiry (L2).

97
00:07:40.619 --> 00:07:41.941
Design the read path:

98
00:07:42.221 --> 00:07:49.155
301 versus 302, caching layers, hot keys and campaign spikes (L2, L3).

99
00:07:50.505 --> 00:07:55.945
Design click analytics that never slows a redirect, including unique counts (L4).

100
00:07:56.225 --> 00:08:02.817
Handle abuse, privacy and lifecycle: phishing, enumeration, bots, deletion and data retention.

101
00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:05.041
Part 2.

102
00:08:05.321 --> 00:08:07.199
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

103
00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:10.603
Intuition: a coat-check counter.

104
00:08:10.883 --> 00:08:12.910
A short link is a coat-check ticket.

105
00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:17.585
You hand over a long URL (the coat) and get a small ticket (the code).

106
00:08:17.865 --> 00:08:20.446
Anyone holding the ticket can collect the coat.

107
00:08:20.726 --> 00:08:21.409
So:

108
00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:23.075
Tickets must be unique.

109
00:08:23.355 --> 00:08:25.745
Two coats on one ticket is a disaster.

110
00:08:26.025 --> 00:08:27.326
Tickets should be short.

111
00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:30.529
They go into 160-character SMS.

112
00:08:30.809 --> 00:08:33.753
Tickets should not be guessable if the coats are personal.

113
00:08:34.033 --> 00:08:37.553
Ticket 41 shouldn't tell you that ticket 42 exists.

114
00:08:37.833 --> 00:08:42.931
The counter must be fast at returning coats, because far more people collect than deposit.

115
00:08:44.281 --> 00:08:50.361
Old coats are eventually cleared out, and a cleared ticket must say gone, not hand out someone else's coat.

116
00:08:51.711 --> 00:08:52.671
Requirements.

117
00:08:52.951 --> 00:08:53.805
Functional.

118
00:08:54.085 --> 00:09:02.234
Create a short link for a long URL, with owner, optional custom alias and optional expiry; bulk-create for campaigns.

119
00:09:02.514 --> 00:09:05.287
Redirect GET /code to the long URL.

120
00:09:05.567 --> 00:09:08.213
Expire and revoke links; report abuse.

121
00:09:08.493 --> 00:09:14.679
Click analytics per link and campaign: total clicks, unique visitors, by time, device and region.

122
00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:16.069
Non-functional.

123
00:09:17.419 --> 00:09:25.248
Redirect latency: p99 under 20 ms server-side; the redirect is on the path of every SMS tap.

124
00:09:25.528 --> 00:09:26.595
Availability:

125
00:09:26.875 --> 00:09:33.147
99.95% for redirects, since a broken tracking link becomes a support call.

126
00:09:33.427 --> 00:09:37.651
Creation can be less available, because notification sends can retry.

127
00:09:37.931 --> 00:09:42.731
Scale: see §2.3; spikes from campaigns matter more than averages.

128
00:09:43.011 --> 00:09:51.331
Security: destinations limited to allow-listed domains; codes not enumerable; no personal data in codes or URLs.

129
00:09:51.611 --> 00:09:56.795
Durability: a link must not disappear or change destination before it expires.

130
00:09:58.145 --> 00:10:04.331
Out of scope (say so in an interview): link-in-bio pages, QR design, a public consumer shortener.

131
00:10:05.681 --> 00:10:06.727
Estimation.

132
00:10:07.007 --> 00:10:08.948
Using Ch 02's method:

133
00:10:10.298 --> 00:10:11.642
Here is a quick table:

134
00:10:11.922 --> 00:10:13.351
Quantity, and Estimate.

135
00:10:13.631 --> 00:10:14.869
Transactional links:

136
00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:18.647
2M per day about 23 creates per second average.

137
00:10:18.927 --> 00:10:20.357
Personalised campaign:

138
00:10:20.637 --> 00:10:26.717
20M links in 1 hour about 5,600 creates per second (bulk API).

139
00:10:26.997 --> 00:10:36.469
Redirects, normal: ~3 clicks per tracking link about 6M per day about 70 per second average, a few thousand per second at evening peak.

140
00:10:36.749 --> 00:10:38.114
Redirects, campaign:

141
00:10:38.394 --> 00:10:47.695
20M SMS times 12% click-through, decaying over ~10 min, then ~30,000 per second peak on one code (L3).

142
00:10:47.975 --> 00:10:57.618
Row size: code 7 B + URL ~150 B + owner, timestamps, hash about ~300 B with index overhead.

143
00:10:57.898 --> 00:10:58.965
Live rows:

144
00:10:59.245 --> 00:11:10.402
2M times 30 d + ~8M per day campaign times 90 d about ~780M rows about 230 GB, bounded by expiry.

145
00:11:11.752 --> 00:11:12.733
Code space:

146
00:11:13.013 --> 00:11:19.691
62^7 about 3.5 trillion; at 2M per day that lasts thousands of years (L1).

147
00:11:21.041 --> 00:11:22.982
Three conclusions shape the design.

148
00:11:23.262 --> 00:11:28.681
Reads dominate and are spiky, so the read path is cache-first and must survive hot keys.

149
00:11:28.961 --> 00:11:39.755
Storage is modest because links expire, so a single well-run relational database (partitioned) is enough, no need to reach for a wide-column store by reflex.

150
00:11:40.035 --> 00:11:43.769
Bulk creation needs a batch API and cheap code minting.

151
00:11:45.119 --> 00:11:46.079
API.

152
00:11:46.359 --> 00:11:48.129
Here is the HTTP code.

153
00:11:50.979 --> 00:11:54.158
The redirect endpoint is public and unauthenticated.

154
00:11:54.438 --> 00:12:02.694
The management API is internal, authenticated per service (Ch 23), and rate-limited per owner (Ch 39).

155
00:12:04.044 --> 00:12:05.303
Generating codes.

156
00:12:05.583 --> 00:12:08.569
L1 compares four schemes over 2 million codes:

157
00:12:09.919 --> 00:12:11.263
Here is a quick table:

158
00:12:11.543 --> 00:12:16.642
Scheme, and Length, and Collisions, and Uniqueness check per insert, and Guessable neighbours.

159
00:12:16.922 --> 00:12:20.165
A) Hash of URL, truncated to 6 base62.

160
00:12:20.445 --> 00:12:21.149
Length:

161
00:12:21.429 --> 00:12:22.175
6.

162
00:12:22.455 --> 00:12:23.394
Collisions:

163
00:12:23.674 --> 00:12:26.063
40 (birthday bound about 35).

164
00:12:26.343 --> 00:12:27.922
Uniqueness check per insert:

165
00:12:28.202 --> 00:12:30.037
Yes (must detect and retry).

166
00:12:30.317 --> 00:12:31.511
Guessable neighbours:

167
00:12:31.791 --> 00:12:32.474
No.

168
00:12:32.754 --> 00:12:35.911
B) Random 7 base62, check-and-retry.

169
00:12:36.191 --> 00:12:36.895
Length:

170
00:12:37.175 --> 00:12:37.922
7.

171
00:12:38.202 --> 00:12:39.141
Collisions:

172
00:12:39.421 --> 00:12:40.935
0 (after retry).

173
00:12:41.215 --> 00:12:42.794
Uniqueness check per insert:

174
00:12:43.074 --> 00:12:44.461
Yes, every insert.

175
00:12:44.741 --> 00:12:45.935
Guessable neighbours:

176
00:12:46.215 --> 00:12:46.898
No.

177
00:12:47.178 --> 00:12:49.311
C) Counter, then base62.

178
00:12:49.591 --> 00:12:50.295
Length:

179
00:12:50.575 --> 00:12:51.343
5.

180
00:12:51.623 --> 00:12:52.562
Collisions:

181
00:12:52.842 --> 00:12:53.589
0.

182
00:12:53.869 --> 00:12:55.447
Uniqueness check per insert:

183
00:12:55.727 --> 00:12:56.410
No.

184
00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:57.885
Guessable neighbours:

185
00:12:58.165 --> 00:12:59.423
100%.

186
00:12:59.703 --> 00:13:03.223
D) Counter, then keyed permutation, then base62.

187
00:13:03.503 --> 00:13:04.207
Length:

188
00:13:04.487 --> 00:13:05.234
7.

189
00:13:05.514 --> 00:13:06.453
Collisions:

190
00:13:06.733 --> 00:13:07.479
0.

191
00:13:07.759 --> 00:13:09.338
Uniqueness check per insert:

192
00:13:09.618 --> 00:13:10.301
No.

193
00:13:10.581 --> 00:13:11.775
Guessable neighbours:

194
00:13:12.055 --> 00:13:14.082
0.00%.

195
00:13:15.432 --> 00:13:22.259
Hash truncation is attractive because it needs no coordination and dedupes identical URLs for free.

196
00:13:22.539 --> 00:13:30.411
But collisions grow with n² or 2N, 40 in 2M codes, millions per year at scale, so you still need check-and-retry.

197
00:13:30.691 --> 00:13:37.709
And same URL, then same code is wrong across owners anyway (different expiry, analytics, revocation).

198
00:13:37.989 --> 00:13:48.037
Random codes are simple and unguessable, and collisions stay rare while the space is sparse (0.00006% full in L1).

199
00:13:48.317 --> 00:13:53.309
The cost is a uniqueness check on every insert, a unique index and a retry loop.

200
00:13:53.589 --> 00:13:55.232
That's fine for most systems.

201
00:13:56.582 --> 00:14:00.465
Counters are perfect for the database: unique, short, no lookup.

202
00:14:00.745 --> 00:14:04.627
They are a privacy failure for personal links (§1.1).

203
00:14:04.907 --> 00:14:09.494
Counter + permutation keeps the counter's properties and removes the ordering.

204
00:14:09.774 --> 00:14:17.945
A Feistel network with secret round keys is a bijection on the 62^7 space; cycle-walking keeps outputs inside it.

205
00:14:18.225 --> 00:14:27.974
Two consecutive counter values map to unrelated-looking codes (jFF7XOx, jstj0mT, pt1IVd8...).

206
00:14:29.324 --> 00:14:40.524
Getting counter values without a bottleneck: each node reserves a block (L2: one nextval on a sequence per 1,000 codes, 11 round-trips for 10,003 codes).

207
00:14:40.804 --> 00:14:51.257
Alternatives are a Snowflake-style id (Ch 20), whose 64 bits are too long for 7 characters, or ranges leased from ZooKeeper or etcd.

208
00:14:51.537 --> 00:14:57.553
Blocks waste unused values when a node restarts, irrelevant in a 3.5-trillion space.

209
00:14:58.903 --> 00:14:59.650
Warning:

210
00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:02.639
A permutation hides order, not existence.

211
00:15:02.919 --> 00:15:10.322
Codes from a 3.5-trillion space with a few hundred million live links are sparse, so random guessing rarely hits.

212
00:15:10.602 --> 00:15:13.162
But rarely isn't never at millions of guesses.

213
00:15:13.442 --> 00:15:25.773
Rate-limit 404s per client (Ch 39), and never let a short code alone unlock personal data: the tracking page behind it still requires a signed token or login.

214
00:15:27.123 --> 00:15:28.104
The write path.

215
00:15:28.384 --> 00:15:31.093
Authenticate the calling service; check its quota.

216
00:15:31.373 --> 00:15:50.083
Validate the destination (L2): parse it with a real URL parser, require https, reject user-info (https://grocery.in@evil.example points at evil.example), and require an allow-listed host, exact match, not contains grocery.

217
00:15:50.363 --> 00:15:53.883
Optionally scan with a safe-browsing service for partner links.

218
00:15:55.233 --> 00:15:59.073
Idempotency: the notification service retries on timeouts.

219
00:15:59.353 --> 00:16:13.134
Either honour an Idempotency-Key header or use a natural key, L2 uses a unique (owner, sha256(url)) constraint with insert... on conflict do nothing, then reads the existing code.

220
00:16:13.414 --> 00:16:17.510
A retry returns 200 with the same code, never a second link.

221
00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:27.774
Mint the code (§2.5) or reserve a custom alias (checked against a reserved-word list: api, admin, login, brand names, profanity).

222
00:16:28.054 --> 00:16:32.342
Store with owner, created or expiry timestamps and campaign id.

223
00:16:32.622 --> 00:16:36.313
Warm the cache for campaign links created ahead of a send (L3).

224
00:16:37.663 --> 00:16:38.665
The read path:

225
00:16:38.945 --> 00:16:41.463
301 versus 302.

226
00:16:41.743 --> 00:16:43.087
Here is a quick table:

227
00:16:43.367 --> 00:16:46.332
Status, and Meaning, and Browser caching, and Use.

228
00:16:46.612 --> 00:16:48.767
301 Moved Permanently.

229
00:16:49.047 --> 00:16:49.836
Meaning:

230
00:16:50.116 --> 00:16:51.844
Destination will never change.

231
00:16:52.124 --> 00:16:53.255
Browser caching:

232
00:16:53.535 --> 00:16:56.116
Browsers and proxies may cache indefinitely.

233
00:16:56.396 --> 00:16:57.100
Use:

234
00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.452
Rarely: you lose analytics and the ability to revoke.

235
00:17:00.732 --> 00:17:04.273
302 Found or 307 Temporary.

236
00:17:04.553 --> 00:17:05.343
Meaning:

237
00:17:05.623 --> 00:17:07.116
Destination may change.

238
00:17:07.396 --> 00:17:08.527
Browser caching:

239
00:17:08.807 --> 00:17:10.727
Not cached unless headers say so.

240
00:17:11.007 --> 00:17:11.711
Use:

241
00:17:11.991 --> 00:17:14.999
Default: every click reaches you, revocation works.

242
00:17:15.279 --> 00:17:17.881
308 Permanent (method-preserving).

243
00:17:18.161 --> 00:17:18.951
Meaning:

244
00:17:19.231 --> 00:17:21.172
As 301 for non-GET.

245
00:17:21.452 --> 00:17:22.583
Browser caching:

246
00:17:22.863 --> 00:17:23.652
Cached.

247
00:17:23.932 --> 00:17:24.636
Use:

248
00:17:24.916 --> 00:17:26.217
Not for short links.

249
00:17:26.497 --> 00:17:28.737
404 or 410.

250
00:17:29.017 --> 00:17:29.807
Meaning:

251
00:17:30.087 --> 00:17:31.623
Unknown or gone for good.

252
00:17:31.903 --> 00:17:33.033
Browser caching:

253
00:17:33.313 --> 00:17:34.999
410 may be cached.

254
00:17:35.279 --> 00:17:35.983
Use:

255
00:17:36.263 --> 00:17:38.737
Unknown code or expired or revoked.

256
00:17:40.087 --> 00:17:45.357
L2 returns 302 with Cache-Control: private, max-age=0.

257
00:17:45.637 --> 00:17:52.527
The §1.1 analytics gap came from 301s: browsers served repeat clicks from cache.

258
00:17:52.807 --> 00:17:54.386
The trade-off is explicit:

259
00:17:54.666 --> 00:17:58.911
302 means you serve every click, so the read path must be cheap.

260
00:17:59.191 --> 00:18:00.578
The read path, in order:

261
00:18:00.858 --> 00:18:08.090
Edge or CDN (optional) for campaign codes, with short TTLs and the redirect logic in an edge function.

262
00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:14.365
In-process near-cache on every redirect node, seconds of TTL, the hot-key defence (L3).

263
00:18:15.715 --> 00:18:20.643
Distributed cache (Redis), minutes to hours of TTL, holding the working set.

264
00:18:20.923 --> 00:18:24.123
Database, the source of truth, reached on cold misses.

265
00:18:24.403 --> 00:18:26.323
Coalesce concurrent misses per key:

266
00:18:26.603 --> 00:18:35.413
L2 saw 716 duplicate reads when many requests missed the same cold, popular code at once (Ch 07).

267
00:18:35.693 --> 00:18:41.709
Negative caching for unknown codes, briefly, so enumeration attempts don't reach the database.

268
00:18:43.059 --> 00:18:44.873
Caching under skew and hot keys.

269
00:18:45.153 --> 00:18:47.457
Link popularity is heavily skewed.

270
00:18:47.737 --> 00:18:55.566
L3 models it as Zipf (s = 1.0, measure your own) and replays 10 million clicks over 1 million links:

271
00:18:56.916 --> 00:18:58.260
Here is a quick table:

272
00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:02.231
LRU size, and % of links, and Hit ratio, and Memory.

273
00:19:02.511 --> 00:19:04.409
1,000. % of links:

274
00:19:04.689 --> 00:19:06.204
0.1%.

275
