278
00:19:10.652 --> 00:19:12.103
0.2 MB.

279
00:19:12.383 --> 00:19:14.260
10,000. % of links:

280
00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:15.521
1%.

281
00:19:15.801 --> 00:19:16.847
Hit ratio:

282
00:19:17.127 --> 00:19:18.897
58.5%.

283
00:19:19.177 --> 00:19:19.967
Memory:

284
00:19:20.247 --> 00:19:21.207
2 MB.

285
00:19:21.487 --> 00:19:23.705
100,000. % of links:

286
00:19:23.985 --> 00:19:24.945
10%.

287
00:19:25.225 --> 00:19:26.271
Hit ratio:

288
00:19:26.551 --> 00:19:28.513
77.7%.

289
00:19:28.793 --> 00:19:29.583
Memory:

290
00:19:29.863 --> 00:19:30.908
20 MB.

291
00:19:31.188 --> 00:19:33.748
250,000. % of links:

292
00:19:34.028 --> 00:19:35.329
25%.

293
00:19:35.609 --> 00:19:36.655
Hit ratio:

294
00:19:36.935 --> 00:19:38.748
85.5%.

295
00:19:39.028 --> 00:19:39.817
Memory:

296
00:19:40.097 --> 00:19:41.207
50 MB.

297
00:19:42.557 --> 00:19:47.698
The curve flattens: the head is cheap to cache, the long tail always reaches the database.

298
00:19:47.978 --> 00:19:51.093
Size the cache for the head and the database for the tail.

299
00:19:51.373 --> 00:19:55.255
A campaign is a different shape: one key takes most of the traffic.

300
00:19:55.535 --> 00:20:09.530
Sharding spreads keys, not load, so every click on the offer code lands on one Redis shard, 27,184 req per second, 49x its peers in L3's 20M-SMS blast.

301
00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:28.327
That is still within one shard's capacity (~27% of ~100k simple GETs per second, VERIFY), but the load scales with the size of the blast, not the cluster: a ~74M-SMS festival campaign would saturate it, and adding shards changes nothing.

302
00:20:28.607 --> 00:20:29.525
Defences:

303
00:20:30.875 --> 00:20:34.757
Near-cache on each node: at most one lookup per node per TTL.

304
00:20:35.037 --> 00:20:40.861
With a 1-second TTL on 20 nodes, the hot shard sees 570 req per second.

305
00:20:41.141 --> 00:20:44.683
Pre-warm scheduled campaigns on every node before the send.

306
00:20:44.963 --> 00:20:49.101
Edge caching of the redirect with a short TTL for campaign codes.

307
00:20:49.381 --> 00:20:59.109
Bulkheads: a separate connection pool and deployment for the redirect path, so a spike can't starve order tracking (§1.1) (Ch 18).

308
00:21:00.459 --> 00:21:02.017
Data model and storage.

309
00:21:02.297 --> 00:21:03.897
Here is the SQL code.

310
00:21:06.747 --> 00:21:13.403
The access pattern is pure key-value (lookup by code), and the live set is ~230 GB.

311
00:21:13.683 --> 00:21:19.869
A partitioned PostgreSQL with a read replica or two, fronted by caches, meets it comfortably.

312
00:21:20.149 --> 00:21:26.293
Partitioning by expiry month turns expiry into DROP PARTITION rather than billions of deletes.

313
00:21:26.573 --> 00:21:29.859
Put permanent (no-expiry) links in their own partition.

314
00:21:30.139 --> 00:21:33.339
When would you choose DynamoDB or Cassandra instead?

315
00:21:33.619 --> 00:21:41.128
When the live set reaches many terabytes, you need multi-region active-active writes, or you want native per-item TTL.

316
00:21:41.408 --> 00:21:44.032
The key-value access pattern ports easily.

317
00:21:44.312 --> 00:21:49.325
In an interview, name the trigger rather than picking by reflex (Ch 06).

318
00:21:50.675 --> 00:21:54.131
Sharding, if needed, is by hash of code (Ch 11).

319
00:21:54.411 --> 00:21:58.913
Because codes come from a permutation, they are already uniformly distributed.

320
00:22:00.263 --> 00:22:01.372
Click analytics.

321
00:22:01.652 --> 00:22:07.049
Marketing wants clicks, unique visitors, time series and breakdowns, in near real time.

322
00:22:07.329 --> 00:22:10.657
The iron rule: the redirect must not wait for analytics.

323
00:22:10.937 --> 00:22:17.423
L4 measured three ways to count 40,000 clicks against PostgreSQL with durable commits:

324
00:22:18.773 --> 00:22:20.117
Here is a quick table:

325
00:22:20.397 --> 00:22:26.029
Strategy, and Clicks per second, and p99 cost on the redirect, and DB statements.

326
00:22:26.309 --> 00:22:29.039
UPDATE per click, spread over 1,000 links.

327
00:22:29.319 --> 00:22:35.762
Clicks per second: ~12,000. p99 cost on the redirect: ~11 ms.

328
00:22:36.042 --> 00:22:37.279
DB statements:

329
00:22:37.559 --> 00:22:38.669
40,000.

330
00:22:38.949 --> 00:22:41.381
UPDATE per click, one hot campaign link.

331
00:22:41.661 --> 00:22:49.127
Clicks per second: ~2,300. p99 cost on the redirect: ~67 ms.

332
00:22:49.407 --> 00:22:50.645
DB statements:

333
00:22:50.925 --> 00:22:52.034
40,000.

334
00:22:52.314 --> 00:22:54.703
Buffer + 1 s micro-batch upsert.

335
00:22:54.983 --> 00:22:59.634
Clicks per second: paced 10,000. p99 cost on the redirect:

336
00:22:59.914 --> 00:23:01.791
0.01 ms.

337
00:23:02.071 --> 00:23:04.589
DB statements: ~2,000.

338
00:23:05.939 --> 00:23:09.672
A synchronous counter update puts a row lock on the redirect path.

339
00:23:09.952 --> 00:23:16.779
It degrades exactly when a campaign makes one link hot, because every commit on that row queues behind the previous one.

340
00:23:17.059 --> 00:23:18.040
In production:

341
00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:28.581
The redirect node emits a click event (code, timestamp, hashed device id, user agent, coarse region) to a local buffer, then to Kafka (Ch 14).

342
00:23:28.861 --> 00:23:42.067
A stream aggregator (Flink or Kafka Streams) produces per-minute counts per link and campaign into an analytics store (ClickHouse, Druid or Pinot), and the raw events land in the lake for ad-hoc analysis.

343
00:23:43.417 --> 00:23:57.027
Unique visitors use HyperLogLog: unique counts don't add across nodes or time windows (L4: adding 20 per-node counts overstated uniques by 294%), but HLL sketches merge.

344
00:23:57.307 --> 00:24:06.950
L4's 16 KB sketches came within +0.41% (standard error 0.81% at p = 14).

345
00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:13.011
Redis PFADD or PFMERGE and ClickHouse uniqHLL12 implement the same idea.

346
00:24:13.291 --> 00:24:21.334
Bot filtering: link-preview crawlers (WhatsApp, Slack, iMessage), security scanners and prefetchers fetch links without a human.

347
00:24:21.614 --> 00:24:28.227
Identify them by user agent and behaviour (HEAD requests, no JS beacon) and count them separately.

348
00:24:28.507 --> 00:24:30.726
Don't block them, previews help click-through.

349
00:24:32.076 --> 00:24:35.745
Counts are seconds behind real time; say so on the dashboard.

350
00:24:36.025 --> 00:24:43.023
For money-related attribution (affiliate payouts), reconcile from raw events, not from the real-time counters.

351
00:24:44.373 --> 00:24:46.442
Abuse, privacy and lifecycle.

352
00:24:46.722 --> 00:25:03.959
Phishing and open redirects: allow-listed destination hosts, parsed properly (L2); no public creation; partner links scanned and reviewed; an abuse-report endpoint and a kill switch that revokes a code everywhere, including caches (publish invalidation events).

353
00:25:04.239 --> 00:25:13.071
Enumeration: permuted codes (L1), rate limits on 404s, negative caching, and nothing sensitive behind a code alone.

354
00:25:14.421 --> 00:25:21.205
Personal data: never put phone numbers, names or order details in the long URL's query string in clear.

355
00:25:21.485 --> 00:25:23.960
Use opaque tokens resolved server-side.

356
00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:31.237
Click events store a salted hash of the device id, with retention limits and erasure support (Ch 36).

357
00:25:31.517 --> 00:25:32.499
Expiry:

358
00:25:32.779 --> 00:25:34.869
410 after expiry.

359
00:25:35.149 --> 00:25:42.872
Don't recycle codes for at least the retention period of any system that might still hold them (SMS archives, screenshots).

360
00:25:43.152 --> 00:25:47.419
With a 3.5-trillion space there is no reason to recycle at all.

361
00:25:47.699 --> 00:25:55.037
Revocation: a revokedat column plus a cache-invalidation event; near-caches' short TTLs bound the staleness.

362
00:25:56.387 --> 00:25:58.478
Multi-region and availability.

363
00:25:58.758 --> 00:26:06.737
Reads are replicated to every serving region; a region can serve redirects from its cache and replica even if the primary is down.

364
00:26:07.017 --> 00:26:13.353
Stale reads are acceptable for links created seconds ago, except for the creator's own immediate test click.

365
00:26:13.633 --> 00:26:22.102
Handle that with read-your-writes, by routing the first lookup of a fresh code to the primary, or by writing through the cache on create (Ch 10).

366
00:26:22.382 --> 00:26:28.313
Writes go to one primary region, with the second region as warm standby (Ch 27).

367
00:26:28.593 --> 00:26:35.931
Because counter blocks are reserved, the standby's minting just continues from the sequence after failover, without collisions.

368
00:26:37.281 --> 00:26:50.209
Degraded mode: if the database is unavailable, redirects continue from caches for the working set; creation fails fast and callers retry (notifications can send without a link as a last resort).

369
00:26:50.489 --> 00:26:58.319
SLOs: redirect availability and latency per region; creation success rate; analytics freshness (Ch 24).

370
00:26:59.669 --> 00:27:00.799
Interview framing.

371
00:27:01.079 --> 00:27:03.639
In a 45-minute interview, spend roughly:

372
00:27:03.919 --> 00:27:16.122
5 min requirements, 5 min estimation, 5 min API and data model, 10 min high-level design, 15 min on two deep dives, and 5 min on failure modes and evolution.

373
00:27:16.402 --> 00:27:26.599
The strongest deep dives are code generation (show the trade-off table, not one scheme) and hot keys or analytics (show you know the redirect path must stay cheap).

374
00:27:26.879 --> 00:27:41.578
Staff-level signals: bounded storage via expiry, abuse and privacy as first-class requirements, choosing PostgreSQL or a KV store with a stated trigger, and bulkheads between campaign spikes and transactional links.

