00:46:36.466 --> 00:46:38.642
COUNTING CLICKS WITHOUT HURTING REDIRECTS.

567
00:46:38.922 --> 00:46:43.231
Marketing wants clicks and UNIQUE visitors per link, per campaign, in near.

568
00:46:46.081 --> 00:46:46.999
On screen:

569
00:46:47.279 --> 00:46:54.191
Part 1, 40,000 clicks, 32 concurrent writers, PostgreSQL 16 with durable commits.

570
00:46:54.471 --> 00:47:00.721
(fsync on). strategy clicks per second p99 cost on redirect DB statements counted.

571
00:47:01.001 --> 00:47:10.345
A) UPDATE per click, spread over 1,000 links 12,253 11.33 ms 40,000 40,000.

572
00:47:10.625 --> 00:47:19.905
B) UPDATE per click, ONE hot campaign link 2,263 67.22 ms 40,000 40,000.

573
00:47:20.185 --> 00:47:30.660
C) buffer + 1 s micro-batch upsert (half on hot link) 10,000 0.0100 ms 2,029 40,000.

574
00:47:30.940 --> 00:47:37.063
(C is paced at 10,000 clicks per second, its limit is the redirect traffic, not the. counter).

575
00:47:37.343 --> 00:47:51.252
Part 2, 3,000,000 clicks from repeat visitors across 20 redirect nodes. exact unique devices (HashSet) 663,668 ~37 MB of set entries.

576
00:47:51.532 --> 00:47:53.047
The rest is in the chapter.

577
00:47:55.897 --> 00:47:56.750
Reading it:

578
00:47:57.030 --> 00:48:05.414
Spread-out updates manage ~12,000 per second with an ~11 ms p99, already a cost on every redirect.

579
00:48:05.694 --> 00:48:16.979
On one hot row throughput drops to ~2,300 per second and p99 to ~67 ms: each commit on the row waits for the previous one to flush.

580
00:48:17.259 --> 00:48:28.651
Micro-batching makes the redirect's cost a 0.01 ms memory increment and writes ~2,000 upserts (one per link per flush) instead of 40,000.

581
00:48:28.931 --> 00:48:33.411
All 40,000 clicks were counted, including the final flush on shutdown.

582
00:48:34.761 --> 00:48:54.004
Unique visitors: merged HLL sketches estimated 666,410 against an exact 663,668 (+0.41%, inside the 0.81% standard error) in 16 KB per sketch.

583
00:48:54.284 --> 00:49:02.817
Adding per-node unique counts gave 2.6 million (+294%), because the same device clicks through many nodes.

584
00:49:03.097 --> 00:49:07.236
In production the buffer is a Kafka topic and the flusher a stream job.

585
00:49:07.516 --> 00:49:12.529
The principle is the same: counts are a second behind, and the redirect never waits.

586
00:49:13.879 --> 00:49:14.818
Part 6.

587
00:49:15.098 --> 00:49:16.378
Production Concerns.

588
00:49:17.628 --> 00:49:18.780
Failure modes.

589
00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:20.404
Here is a quick table:

590
00:49:20.684 --> 00:49:22.967
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

591
00:49:23.247 --> 00:49:24.505
Sequential codes.

592
00:49:24.785 --> 00:49:25.596
Symptom:

593
00:49:25.876 --> 00:49:27.988
Neighbouring links enumerable (L1).

594
00:49:28.268 --> 00:49:29.271
Mitigation:

595
00:49:29.551 --> 00:49:33.135
Keyed permutation; no personal data behind code alone.

596
00:49:33.415 --> 00:49:35.100
301 redirects.

597
00:49:35.380 --> 00:49:36.191
Symptom:

598
00:49:36.471 --> 00:49:38.156
Missing clicks; can't revoke.

599
00:49:38.436 --> 00:49:39.439
Mitigation:

600
00:49:39.719 --> 00:49:42.919
302 or 307 with no-store.

601
00:49:43.199 --> 00:49:44.415
Campaign hot key.

602
00:49:44.695 --> 00:49:45.505
Symptom:

603
00:49:45.785 --> 00:49:49.817
One shard or row saturated; shared pool exhausted (L3).

604
00:49:50.097 --> 00:49:51.100
Mitigation:

605
00:49:51.380 --> 00:49:53.748
Near-cache, pre-warm, edge, bulkheads.

606
00:49:54.028 --> 00:49:55.329
Cold-miss stampede.

607
00:49:55.609 --> 00:49:56.420
Symptom:

608
00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.812
Duplicate DB reads (L2:

609
00:49:59.092 --> 00:50:00.500
716).

610
00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:01.783
Mitigation:

611
00:50:02.063 --> 00:50:03.449
Per-key miss coalescing.

612
00:50:03.729 --> 00:50:05.031
Synchronous counters.

613
00:50:05.311 --> 00:50:06.121
Symptom:

614
00:50:06.401 --> 00:50:09.409
Redirect latency tied to row locks (L4).

615
00:50:09.689 --> 00:50:10.692
Mitigation:

616
00:50:10.972 --> 00:50:12.849
Async events + aggregation.

617
00:50:14.199 --> 00:50:15.927
Unrestricted destinations.

618
00:50:16.207 --> 00:50:17.018
Symptom:

619
00:50:17.298 --> 00:50:18.962
Phishing on the trusted domain.

620
00:50:19.242 --> 00:50:20.245
Mitigation:

621
00:50:20.525 --> 00:50:23.959
Allow-list, parser-based validation, abuse kill switch.

622
00:50:24.239 --> 00:50:25.690
Mass expiry deletes.

623
00:50:25.970 --> 00:50:26.781
Symptom:

624
00:50:27.061 --> 00:50:29.045
Vacuum storms, replica lag.

625
00:50:29.325 --> 00:50:30.327
Mitigation:

626
00:50:30.607 --> 00:50:33.167
Partition by expiry, drop partitions.

627
00:50:33.447 --> 00:50:34.535
Bot traffic.

628
00:50:34.815 --> 00:50:35.626
Symptom:

629
00:50:35.906 --> 00:50:37.058
Inflated clicks.

630
00:50:37.338 --> 00:50:38.341
Mitigation:

631
00:50:38.621 --> 00:50:41.330
UA or behaviour filtering, separate counts.

632
00:50:42.680 --> 00:50:43.768
Observability.

633
00:50:44.048 --> 00:50:54.181
RED metrics on the redirect path, per region and per status (302 or 404 or 410), plus cache hit ratio per tier.

634
00:50:54.461 --> 00:51:01.501
Hot-key detection: top codes per node per minute (count-min sketch), alerting when one code exceeds a share of traffic.

635
00:51:01.781 --> 00:51:05.237
404 rate per client as an enumeration signal.

636
00:51:05.517 --> 00:51:08.973
Analytics lag: event time versus aggregation time.

637
00:51:09.253 --> 00:51:19.216
Creation: idempotent replays (200s), validation rejections by reason, a spike in rejections may be an attack or a misconfigured campaign.

638
00:51:20.566 --> 00:51:21.505
Security.

639
00:51:21.785 --> 00:51:27.865
Destination allow-list managed as code, reviewed changes, audit log (Ch 36).

640
00:51:28.145 --> 00:51:33.201
Management API: mTLS or service identity, per-owner quotas.

641
00:51:33.481 --> 00:51:40.478
Permutation keys rotate only for new codes (old codes stay valid, the mapping is only used at mint time).

642
00:51:40.758 --> 00:51:45.985
Tracking pages behind links require a signed token bound to the order and expiry.

643
00:51:47.335 --> 00:51:48.231
Performance.

644
00:51:48.511 --> 00:51:54.612
The redirect handler does no allocation-heavy work: a near-cache lookup, a header, an async enqueue.

645
00:51:54.892 --> 00:51:59.521
It's a good fit for virtual threads or a non-blocking server (Ch 19).

646
00:51:59.801 --> 00:52:06.223
Keep responses tiny: no body on 302, keep-alive, HTTP or 2 at the edge.

647
00:52:06.503 --> 00:52:14.183
Bulk creation: batch inserts (L2 inserted 10,000 in one batch), pre-reserved counter blocks, async results.

648
00:52:15.533 --> 00:52:16.258
Cost.

649
00:52:16.538 --> 00:52:23.151
Storage is small and bounded by expiry; the main costs are the redirect fleet and the analytics pipeline.

650
00:52:23.431 --> 00:52:27.485
Edge caching of campaign codes is the cheapest capacity for spikes.

651
00:52:27.765 --> 00:52:34.847
Keep raw click events for a limited period (for example, 90 days) and aggregates longer (Ch 35).

652
00:52:36.197 --> 00:52:37.136
Part 7.

653
00:52:37.416 --> 00:52:39.123
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

654
00:52:40.373 --> 00:52:41.290
Watch out:

655
00:52:41.570 --> 00:52:42.210
1.

656
00:52:42.490 --> 00:52:44.986
Auto-increment ids as public codes.

657
00:52:45.266 --> 00:52:47.719
Every link reveals its neighbours (L1).

658
00:52:47.999 --> 00:52:49.941
Permute, or use random codes.

659
00:52:51.291 --> 00:52:52.208
Watch out:

660
00:52:52.488 --> 00:52:53.192
2.

661
00:52:53.472 --> 00:52:55.883
Hash truncation without collision handling.

662
00:52:56.163 --> 00:53:02.755
Birthday collisions arrive far sooner than intuition says, 40 in 2 million at 6 characters (L1).

663
00:53:04.105 --> 00:53:05.022
Watch out:

664
00:53:05.302 --> 00:53:06.049
3.

665
00:53:06.329 --> 00:53:09.635
Deduplicating identical URLs across owners.

666
00:53:09.915 --> 00:53:13.755
Different owners need different expiry, analytics and revocation.

667
00:53:14.035 --> 00:53:15.721
Dedupe per owner, if at all.

668
00:53:17.071 --> 00:53:17.988
Watch out:

669
00:53:18.268 --> 00:53:18.972
4.

670
00:53:19.252 --> 00:53:20.916
301 by default.

671
00:53:21.196 --> 00:53:26.529
Browser caching hides clicks and makes revocation impossible for those who've clicked before.

672
00:53:27.879 --> 00:53:28.797
Watch out:

673
00:53:29.077 --> 00:53:29.845
5.

674
00:53:30.125 --> 00:53:39.703
Validating destinations with string matching. contains(grocery.in) accepts grocery.in.evil.example and grocery.in@evil.example.

675
00:53:39.983 --> 00:53:42.799
Parse, then compare hosts exactly (L2).

676
00:53:44.149 --> 00:53:45.067
Watch out:

677
00:53:45.347 --> 00:53:46.093
6.

678
00:53:46.373 --> 00:53:48.827
Public or unauthenticated link creation.

679
00:53:49.107 --> 00:53:51.731
Your trusted domain becomes a phishing service.

680
00:53:53.081 --> 00:53:53.998
Watch out:

681
00:53:54.278 --> 00:53:55.025
7.

682
00:53:55.305 --> 00:53:56.841
Counting clicks synchronously.

683
00:53:57.121 --> 00:54:01.857
A row lock on the redirect path, worst exactly during campaigns (L4).

684
00:54:03.207 --> 00:54:04.124
Watch out:

685
00:54:04.404 --> 00:54:05.087
8.

686
00:54:05.367 --> 00:54:06.689
Summing unique counts.

687
00:54:06.969 --> 00:54:12.537
Uniques don't add across nodes, days or links (L4: +294%).

688
00:54:12.817 --> 00:54:14.332
Use mergeable sketches.

689
00:54:15.682 --> 00:54:16.599
Watch out:

690
00:54:16.879 --> 00:54:17.626
9.

691
00:54:17.906 --> 00:54:20.061
Expecting sharding to fix a hot key.

692
00:54:20.341 --> 00:54:22.410
Sharding spreads keys, not load.

693
00:54:22.690 --> 00:54:25.165
One code lives on one shard (L3).

694
00:54:26.515 --> 00:54:27.432
Watch out:

695
00:54:27.712 --> 00:54:28.416
10.

696
00:54:28.696 --> 00:54:31.533
One pool for campaigns and transactional links.

697
00:54:31.813 --> 00:54:35.547
A campaign spike starves order tracking (§1.1).

698
00:54:35.827 --> 00:54:36.829
Bulkhead them.

699
00:54:38.179 --> 00:54:39.097
Watch out:

700
00:54:39.377 --> 00:54:40.209
11.

701
00:54:40.489 --> 00:54:42.686
Deleting expired rows one by one.

702
00:54:42.966 --> 00:54:46.187
Billions of deletes cause vacuum and replication pain.

703
00:54:46.467 --> 00:54:47.982
Partition by expiry.

704
00:54:49.332 --> 00:54:50.249
Watch out:

705
00:54:50.529 --> 00:54:51.319
12.

706
00:54:51.599 --> 00:54:52.815
Recycling codes.

707
00:54:53.095 --> 00:55:00.625
Old SMS and screenshots still point at them; a recycled code sends someone else's customer to a new destination.

708
00:55:01.975 --> 00:55:02.850
Part 8.

709
00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:04.474
Interview Q and A.

710
00:55:05.724 --> 00:55:06.556
Beginner.

711
00:55:06.836 --> 00:55:07.860
Question 1.

712
00:55:08.140 --> 00:55:11.340
Design a URL shortener, what are the core components?

713
00:55:11.620 --> 00:55:12.985
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:55:13.265 --> 00:55:19.964
A write API that validates the long URL, mints a unique short code and stores code, then URL.

715
00:55:20.244 --> 00:55:27.092
A redirect endpoint that looks up the code, through caches, and returns a 302 to the long URL.

716
00:55:27.372 --> 00:55:34.220
A database as the source of truth, a cache for the hot working set, and an asynchronous pipeline for click analytics.

717
00:55:34.500 --> 00:55:37.359
Plus expiry, abuse controls and monitoring.

718
00:55:38.709 --> 00:55:39.711
Question 2.

719
00:55:39.991 --> 00:55:43.789
301 or 302, which would you use and why?

720
00:55:44.069 --> 00:55:45.434
Thirty-second answer:

721
00:55:45.714 --> 00:55:48.807
302 (or 307) by default.

722
00:55:49.087 --> 00:56:03.061
A 301 says the redirect is permanent, so browsers and proxies may cache it, repeat clicks never reach you, analytics undercount, and you can't revoke or change the destination for anyone who has the cached redirect.

723
00:56:03.341 --> 00:56:09.357
A 302 means every click reaches the service, so the read path has to be cheap and well-cached.

724
00:56:10.707 --> 00:56:11.752
Question 3.

725
00:56:12.032 --> 00:56:14.272
How many characters does a short code need?

726
00:56:14.552 --> 00:56:15.917
Thirty-second answer:

727
00:56:16.197 --> 00:56:19.781
Work it out from the number of codes you'll ever mint and the alphabet.

728
00:56:20.061 --> 00:56:27.784
Base62 gives 62^6 about 56.8 billion and 62^7 about 3.5 trillion.

729
00:56:28.064 --> 00:56:37.387
At 2M links per day, 7 characters lasts thousands of years; 6 would last about 78 years but random schemes would collide much sooner.

730
00:56:37.667 --> 00:56:43.085
For random codes you also want the space sparse enough that guesses rarely hit a live code.

731
00:56:44.435 --> 00:56:45.502
Question 4.

732
00:56:45.782 --> 00:56:47.958
How do you estimate the load for a shortener?

733
00:56:48.238 --> 00:56:49.603
Thirty-second answer:

734
00:56:49.883 --> 00:57:12.369
Creates per day from the business (2M transactional links per day about 23 per second, plus bulk campaigns up to ~5,600 per second), clicks per link (~3) for average redirects, and the campaign spike for peak (20M SMS times 12% click-through, decaying over ~10 minutes about 30k per second on one code).

735
00:57:12.649 --> 00:57:23.529
Storage = live rows times row size, bounded by expiry: ~780M rows times ~300 B about 230 GB.

736
00:57:24.879 --> 00:57:25.881
Intermediate.

737
00:57:26.161 --> 00:57:27.271
Question 5.

738
00:57:27.551 --> 00:57:29.684
Compare ways to generate short codes.

739
00:57:29.964 --> 00:57:31.329
Thirty-second answer:

740
00:57:31.609 --> 00:57:37.860
Hash-and-truncate needs no coordination but collides (birthday bound) and still needs check-and-retry.

741
00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:42.087
Random codes are unguessable but need a uniqueness check per insert.

742
00:57:42.367 --> 00:57:46.079
A counter is unique and short with no lookup but is enumerable.

743
00:57:46.359 --> 00:57:52.972
A counter through a keyed permutation keeps the counter's uniqueness and no-lookup property while hiding the order.

744
00:57:54.322 --> 00:57:55.431
Question 6.

745
00:57:55.711 --> 00:57:57.951
How do you make link creation idempotent?

746
00:57:58.231 --> 00:57:59.597
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:57:59.877 --> 00:58:07.514
Either an Idempotency-Key header stored with the result, or a natural key: a unique constraint on (owner, hash(url)).

748
00:58:07.794 --> 00:58:14.727
Insert with on conflict do nothing; if nothing was inserted, read and return the existing code with 200.

749
00:58:15.007 --> 00:58:17.205
Retries then never mint a second link.

750
00:58:18.555 --> 00:58:19.643
Question 7.

751
00:58:19.923 --> 00:58:22.397
How do you stop the shortener being used for phishing?

752
00:58:22.677 --> 00:58:24.043
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:58:24.323 --> 00:58:47.107
No public creation; authenticated services with quotas; destinations restricted to allow-listed hosts, validated by parsing the URL (https only, no user-info, exact host match), not string matching; safe-browsing scans for partner links; an abuse report endpoint and a kill switch that revokes a code and invalidates caches.

754
00:58:48.457 --> 00:58:49.481
Question 8.

755
00:58:49.761 --> 00:58:51.382
How would you cache redirects?

756
00:58:51.662 --> 00:58:53.027
Thirty-second answer:

757
00:58:53.307 --> 00:59:08.177
Three tiers: a short-TTL in-process near-cache on each redirect node, a distributed cache (Redis) for the working set, and the database for cold misses, with per-key miss coalescing and brief negative caching of unknown codes.

758
00:59:08.457 --> 00:59:14.750
Popularity is skewed, so a small cache captures most traffic; the tail always reaches the database.

759
00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:17.167
Question 9.

760
00:59:17.447 --> 00:59:20.092
SQL or NoSQL for the links table?

761
00:59:20.372 --> 00:59:21.737
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:59:22.017 --> 00:59:23.148
Estimate first.

763
00:59:23.428 --> 00:59:37.103
With expiry the live set is ~230 GB of pure key-value lookups, which partitioned PostgreSQL with read replicas and caches serves comfortably, and it gives unique constraints for idempotency.

764
00:59:37.383 --> 00:59:49.671
Move to DynamoDB or Cassandra if the live set grows to many terabytes, if you need multi-region active-active writes, or if you want native per-item TTL at huge scale.

765
00:59:51.021 --> 00:59:51.789
Senior.

766
00:59:52.069 --> 00:59:53.114
Question 10.

767
00:59:53.394 --> 00:59:56.317
A campaign sends 20M SMS with one link.

768
00:59:56.597 --> 00:59:58.645
What breaks and how do you design for it?

769
00:59:58.925 --> 01:00:00.290
Thirty-second answer:

770
01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:08.271
All clicks hit one key: one Redis shard, one DB row if you count synchronously, and possibly a shared connection pool.

771
01:00:08.551 --> 01:00:11.581
Sharding doesn't help because it spreads keys, not load.

772
01:00:11.861 --> 01:00:26.815
Defend with a near-cache on every node (at most one lookup per node per TTL), pre-warming, edge caching for campaign codes, async click counting, and a separate deployment or pool for redirects so transactional links are unaffected.

773
01:00:28.165 --> 01:00:29.296
Question 11.

774
01:00:29.576 --> 01:00:32.627
How do you build click analytics without slowing redirects?

775
01:00:32.907 --> 01:00:34.272
Thirty-second answer:

776
01:00:34.552 --> 01:00:38.307
The redirect only emits an event (buffered, async) to Kafka.

777
01:00:38.587 --> 01:00:45.029
A stream processor aggregates per link and time bucket into an analytics store, and raw events go to the lake.

778
01:00:45.309 --> 01:00:50.365
Unique visitors use HyperLogLog sketches, which merge across nodes and windows.

779
01:00:50.645 --> 01:00:54.315
Bots and link-preview crawlers are flagged and counted separately.

780
01:00:55.665 --> 01:00:56.753
Question 12.

781
01:00:57.033 --> 01:00:59.721
How do you handle expiry and deletion at scale?

782
01:01:00.001 --> 01:01:01.366
Thirty-second answer:

783
01:01:01.646 --> 01:01:14.510
Store expiresat, return 410 after it (checked on read, so expiry is exact without a sweeper), and partition by expiry month so purging is DROP PARTITION after a retention period.

784
01:01:14.790 --> 01:01:18.929
Revocation sets revokedat and publishes a cache invalidation.

785
01:01:19.209 --> 01:01:20.638
Never recycle codes.

786
01:01:21.988 --> 01:01:23.268
Question 13.

787
01:01:23.548 --> 01:01:26.044
How do you prevent enumeration of short links?

788
01:01:26.324 --> 01:01:27.689
Thirty-second answer:

789
01:01:27.969 --> 01:01:49.175
Non-sequential codes (random, or a keyed permutation of a counter), a sparse code space, rate limits and alerts on 404s per client, negative caching so probes don't reach the database, and, most important, nothing sensitive reachable by the code alone: the page behind it requires a signed token or login.

790
01:01:50.525 --> 01:01:51.719
Question 14.

791
01:01:51.999 --> 01:01:55.711
How do you support a bulk campaign of 20M personalised links?

792
01:01:55.991 --> 01:01:57.357
Thirty-second answer:

793
01:01:57.637 --> 01:02:04.634
A batch API that accepts large files or streams, returns a job id, and writes results asynchronously.

794
01:02:04.914 --> 01:02:13.234
Pre-reserve large counter blocks, mint codes in memory, and insert with batched statements or COPY into the right expiry partition.

795
01:02:13.514 --> 01:02:16.245
Warm caches only for the likely-hot subset.

796
01:02:16.525 --> 01:02:19.533
Throttle so transactional creates keep their SLO.

797
01:02:20.883 --> 01:02:21.779
Architect.

798
01:02:22.059 --> 01:02:23.253
Question 15.

799
01:02:23.533 --> 01:02:28.227
Design the short-link service for a national-scale grocery platform, end to end.

800
01:02:28.507 --> 01:02:29.872
Thirty-second answer:

801
01:02:30.152 --> 01:02:49.864
Internal-only creation with allow-listed destinations and idempotent create; 7-char codes from counter blocks through a keyed permutation; 302 redirects; a separate redirect deployment with near-cache, Redis and replicas; PostgreSQL partitioned by expiry; click events to Kafka,

802
01:02:50.144 --> 01:02:59.595
stream-aggregated with HLL uniques; hot-key detection, pre-warming and edge caching for campaigns; abuse kill switch; SLOs per region.

803
01:03:00.945 --> 01:03:02.139
Question 16.

804
01:03:02.419 --> 01:03:06.153
How do you make redirects survive a database or region outage?

805
01:03:06.433 --> 01:03:07.798
Thirty-second answer:

806
01:03:08.078 --> 01:03:18.809
Redirects are read-only and cacheable: serve from near-cache and Redis for the working set, from local replicas for misses, and fail creation fast while the primary is down.

807
01:03:19.089 --> 01:03:24.657
Replicate to a second region with its own caches; route users by DNS or anycast.

808
01:03:24.937 --> 01:03:29.139
Tolerate stale reads except read-your-writes for a creator's first click.

809
01:03:30.489 --> 01:03:31.833
Question 17.

810
01:03:32.113 --> 01:03:34.887
Build versus buy: would you use a SaaS shortener?

811
01:03:35.167 --> 01:03:36.532
Thirty-second answer:

812
01:03:36.812 --> 01:03:40.460
For marketing-only links a SaaS (Bitly-style) can be fine.

813
01:03:40.740 --> 01:03:56.527
For transactional links carrying order tracking, you want a first-party domain, control over destinations and data (DLT whitelisting, privacy, residency), latency and availability you control, and no per-click fees at millions of clicks per day.

814
01:03:56.807 --> 01:04:02.033
Many companies run both: first-party for transactional, SaaS for some marketing.

815
01:04:03.383 --> 01:04:04.557
Question 18.

816
01:04:04.837 --> 01:04:08.442
How would you evolve the code scheme without breaking existing links?

817
01:04:08.722 --> 01:04:10.087
Thirty-second answer:

818
01:04:10.367 --> 01:04:16.341
Codes are forever: existing codes stay resolvable by lookup regardless of how they were minted.

819
01:04:16.621 --> 01:04:23.661
A new scheme (longer codes, new permutation keys, a prefix for a new namespace) applies only to new codes.

820
01:04:23.941 --> 01:04:31.194
Keep the namespaces disjoint (for example, by length or a leading character) so new codes never collide with old ones.

821
01:04:32.544 --> 01:04:33.781
Question 19.

822
01:04:34.061 --> 01:04:36.963
What are the privacy obligations for click analytics?

823
01:04:37.243 --> 01:04:38.608
Thirty-second answer:

824
01:04:38.888 --> 01:04:46.184
Click events contain personal data (IP, device id, possibly linked to a customer via the personalised link).

825
01:04:46.464 --> 01:05:04.683
Minimise: coarse region, salted hashed device ids, no raw IPs beyond short security retention; state the purpose; set retention (raw 90 days, aggregates longer); support erasure; keep data in-region; and never put personal identifiers in URLs.

826
01:05:06.033 --> 01:05:07.142
Question 20.

827
01:05:07.422 --> 01:05:11.177
What would you monitor to know the service is healthy, and what would page you?

828
01:05:11.457 --> 01:05:12.822
Thirty-second answer:

829
01:05:13.102 --> 01:05:25.561
Page on redirect SLO burn (availability and p99 per region), a sudden rise in 5xx or 404s, cache hit ratio collapse, and hot-key saturation.

830
01:05:25.841 --> 01:05:32.390
Ticket-level alerts on creation errors, analytics lag, validation rejection spikes and abuse reports.

831
01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:37.449
Dashboards for top codes, per-client 404 rates and campaign traffic.

832
01:05:38.799 --> 01:05:39.695
Part 9.

833
01:05:39.975 --> 01:05:41.383
Summary and Practice.

834
01:05:42.633 --> 01:05:43.721
Key takeaways.

835
01:05:44.001 --> 01:05:52.598
Estimate first: reads dominate and spike; expiry bounds storage (~230 GB live) (§2.3).

836
01:05:52.878 --> 01:06:03.267
Code generation is a trade-off: hashes collide, random codes need lookups, counters leak order; counter blocks + keyed permutation get the best of each (L1).

837
01:06:03.547 --> 01:06:10.545
Creation is idempotent and validated: natural-key upserts; parse URLs, allow-list hosts (L2).

838
01:06:10.825 --> 01:06:17.545
302 by default: every click reaches you, so the read path must be cheap (§2.7).

839
01:06:18.895 --> 01:06:23.353
Cache the head, size the DB for the tail; coalesce misses (L2, L3).

840
01:06:23.633 --> 01:06:28.241
Hot keys defeat sharding: near-cache, pre-warm, edge, bulkheads (L3).

841
01:06:28.521 --> 01:06:32.425
Analytics off the redirect path; HLL for uniques (L4).

842
01:06:32.705 --> 01:06:37.761
Abuse and privacy are requirements, not afterthoughts (§2.11).

843
01:06:39.111 --> 01:06:40.434
Decision cheat sheet.

844
01:06:40.714 --> 01:06:42.058
Here is a quick table:

845
01:06:42.338 --> 01:06:44.109
Question, and Default answer.

846
01:06:44.389 --> 01:06:45.306
Code length:

847
01:06:45.586 --> 01:06:48.402
7 base62 (3.5T).

848
01:06:48.682 --> 01:06:49.621
Code scheme:

849
01:06:49.901 --> 01:06:55.042
Counter blocks + keyed permutation; random + unique index is also fine.

850
01:06:55.322 --> 01:06:56.538
Redirect status:

851
01:06:56.818 --> 01:07:01.959
302 or 307, no-store; 410 for expired.

852
01:07:02.239 --> 01:07:03.029
Store:

853
01:07:03.309 --> 01:07:08.365
Partitioned PostgreSQL + replicas; KV store past a stated trigger.

854
01:07:08.645 --> 01:07:09.370
Cache:

855
01:07:09.650 --> 01:07:14.151
Near-cache (seconds) + Redis + miss coalescing + negative caching.

856
01:07:14.431 --> 01:07:15.327
Campaigns:

857
01:07:15.607 --> 01:07:18.103
Pre-warm, near-cache, edge, separate pool.

858
01:07:18.383 --> 01:07:19.322
Analytics:

859
01:07:19.602 --> 01:07:24.402
Kafka, then stream aggregation, then OLAP; HLL uniques; bots flagged.

860
01:07:24.682 --> 01:07:25.535
Abuse:

861
01:07:25.815 --> 01:07:30.850
Internal creation only, allow-list, kill switch, 404 rate limits.

862
01:07:32.200 --> 01:07:33.331
Exercises.

863
01:07:33.611 --> 01:07:38.837
(B2) In L1, change the code length for strategy A to 7 characters and re-run.

864
01:07:39.117 --> 01:07:40.525
How many collisions now?

865
01:07:40.805 --> 01:07:43.771
At what number of codes would you expect the first collision?

866
01:07:44.051 --> 01:07:50.259
(B2) Add custom aliases to L2 with a reserved-word list and a 409 on conflict.

867
01:07:50.539 --> 01:08:00.864
(B3) Add per-key miss coalescing (a ConcurrentHashMapString, CompletableFutureLink) to L2 and show database reads fall to the number of distinct links.

868
01:08:02.214 --> 01:08:07.227
(B3) In L3, change the Zipf exponent to 0.8 and 1.2.

869
01:08:07.507 --> 01:08:11.049
How does the cache size needed for 80% hit ratio change?

870
01:08:11.329 --> 01:08:20.246
(B4) Extend L4 with per-hour HLL sketches and compute daily uniques by merging 24 of them; compare with an exact count.

871
01:08:21.596 --> 01:08:22.599
Further reading.

872
01:08:22.879 --> 01:08:29.556
RFC 9110 (HTTP semantics), redirect status codes and caching.

873
01:08:29.836 --> 01:08:31.137
Flajolet et al.

874
01:08:31.417 --> 01:08:39.204
HyperLogLog: the analysis of a near-optimal cardinality estimation algorithm (2007); Heule et al.

875
01:08:39.484 --> 01:08:42.471
HyperLogLog in Practice (2013).

876
01:08:42.751 --> 01:08:49.428
Black and Rogaway, Ciphers with Arbitrary Finite Domains (cycle-walking, Feistel on small domains).

877
01:08:49.708 --> 01:08:56.108
TRAI directions on URL whitelisting in commercial SMS (DLT), check the current version.

878
01:08:56.388 --> 01:08:57.177
OWASP:

879
01:08:57.457 --> 01:09:00.060
